{"name":"Cutlery Polish","hex":"#f4dda5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cutlery","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Sharp cutting utensils.","Eating or serving utensils such as knives, forks, and spoons."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cutlery"},{"term":"polish","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A substance used to polish.","Cleanliness; smoothness, shininess."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To shine; to make a surface very smooth or shiny by rubbing, cleaning, or grinding.","To refine; remove imperfections from."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/polish"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cutlery","blocks":[{"text":"Cutlery are utensils used for serving and eating food at the dining table — originally referring to just knives, whereas forks and spoons were silverware — all part of flatware or tableware, these both encompassing crockery as well. These three implements first appeared together on tables as a set in Britain in the Georgian era. A collected set of silverware is called a canteen of cutlery, typically referring to the polished wooden compartmental case in which they are stored."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Besteck_WMF_Stockholm_ca_1960er.jpg/330px-Besteck_WMF_Stockholm_ca_1960er.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cutlery"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Nuovo_Milano_Sottsass.JPG/500px-Nuovo_Milano_Sottsass.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Nuovo_Milano_Sottsass.JPG/1280px-Nuovo_Milano_Sottsass.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Nuovo_Milano_Sottsass.JPG/500px-Nuovo_Milano_Sottsass.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_ca